      {
        "slide": 5,
        "role": "evidence",
        "subject": "One dark asphalt lane on an oblique, with a kerb, gutter and sidewalk on the left held near the asphalt's value and a worn edge line on the right. Three crosswalks cross it one behind another. The farthest has 7 dull brown worn bars (2023 LEASE). The middle has 7 cream bars and one black saw-cut slot in second position with lit kerf lips (ELECTION ORDER). The nearest has 8 cream bars (OCTOBER 13TH). Every row starts at the same kerb offset.",
        "visual_consequence": "Without the headline, a reader counts seven, then seven with one empty slot, then eight. Each row ends inside the frame, so the nearest row is visibly one bar longer. Worn against fresh paint separates the 2023 roll from the two 2026 rolls. The three crosswalks are the only bright shapes on the road, so the eye goes straight to the record. The source's FIG has the same four keys and values, and the counts in the image match them.",
        "weakness": "The image and every native tile match what was accepted earlier today. At 100 percent the bars show a fine thermoplastic speckle and a thin lit lip, but their raised height reads only as that hairline lip with very little cast onto the asphalt. The near and middle rows sit close in value. The kerb and sidewalk wedge in the upper left is a large near-black area, which fits the staged register and is not a blank slab at feed size. The saw-cut slot reads as an empty cut with lit kerf edges, not as an outline.",
